#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537172 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20e3aa6b-07e8-5c44-a5d0-073bb9e6dafd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=5(21-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537146} {:answer/id #uuid "4eca7785-a388-51e6-9aa3-5140075ea5c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=5(452-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537158} {:answer/id #uuid "7e67d3b2-9f17-5e41-9731-8018a73dea2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=2260-B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537175} {:answer/id #uuid "c6f210f7-cd67-501d-8ec0-6733980138d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=(452-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537188} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d53b2e-c3cb-5b93-a2c6-a195e3fa68b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=-5(452-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537199}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537158} :answer-field/id #uuid "7875e0a1-7a16-5786-87e7-5a77a72087b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "79dfba0b-24db-5664-b4c9-d12274cb27d0" :question/problem "A laboratory incubator initially contains $21$ cultures, and its maximum capacity is $452$ cultures. Let $B(t)$ be the number of cultures, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in days. Its population obeys an inhibited growth model. The rate of increase equals $5$ times the difference between the maximum capacity and the current population. Which differential equation models this population?" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of cultures $B(t)$ in the laboratory incubator changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the current number of cultures. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-B),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=452$ cultures, and $k=5$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=5(452-B).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537183 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ca42ad-233a-5895-8bd3-844ba110ab51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=2(102-M)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537141} {:answer/id #uuid "1c04a060-138f-59f7-b590-fdbeca98fb18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=2(140-M)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537144} {:answer/id #uuid "319ab441-06a9-5ffb-a6ea-7c0edcb3f112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=-2(140-M)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537150} {:answer/id #uuid "94565dab-5616-5a0e-8dd7-c18b93bea82b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=280-M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537179} {:answer/id #uuid "ae81661e-4cd7-5065-ac1c-341ae0905ff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=(140-M)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537182}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537144} :answer-field/id #uuid "f81f4355-b278-5458-9f58-703243d83005" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b02bf32a-75d9-5f76-84ee-2472887f212a" :question/problem "A rehabilitation aquarium initially contains $102$ animals, and its maximum capacity is $140$ animals. Let $M(t)$ be the number of animals, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. Its population obeys an inhibited growth model. The rate of increase equals $2$ times the difference between the maximum capacity and the current population. Which differential equation models this population?" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of animals $M(t)$ in the rehabilitation aquarium changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the current number of animals. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-M),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=140$ animals, and $k=2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=2(140-M).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537184 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05e35075-57e6-51be-8856-f3ee3e222472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}(461-N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537134} {:answer/id #uuid "1ae6d362-a710-563a-adf4-609582b42071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=3(461-N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537143} {:answer/id #uuid "29305eee-4969-5796-9ef7-18dd828edaa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}(44-N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537147} {:answer/id #uuid "3ffd4e28-fda9-566f-ad4b-4df3672f98dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{461}{3}-N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537155} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d4712e-335c-588e-887b-9093d0561e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=1(461-N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537198}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537134} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdb7d41c-b943-5c2d-936d-30c48602a24e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b39d9b0e-8fd1-59b4-8d1c-67d4e4ad6fbc" :question/problem "A protected forest initially contains $44$ deer, and its maximum capacity is $461$ deer. Let $N(t)$ be the number of deer, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in years. Its population obeys an inhibited growth model. The rate of increase equals $\\frac{1}{3}$ times the difference between the maximum capacity and the current population. Which differential equation models this population?" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of deer $N(t)$ in the protected forest changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the current number of deer. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-N),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=461$ deer, and $k=\\frac{1}{3}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}(461-N).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dd343d7-f32b-54d0-96a5-f1d029d5aee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}(140-M)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537153} {:answer/id #uuid "6fe74d37-cf56-55fa-b3cb-79ad7cb1d3cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=3(140-M)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537165} {:answer/id #uuid "961009d7-5855-5355-8e46-7ba8a08b1786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{140}{3}-M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537180} {:answer/id #uuid "cd4dc5fc-105e-5447-8915-89d87c2e9105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=1(140-M)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537190} {:answer/id #uuid "fbeb09f7-b984-5d96-b4ed-d14a63d3fb29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}(102-M)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537202}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537153} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf8df66f-845e-5c79-862b-5988ba61c8a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e0319be5-70de-5dd4-b8c9-d544b1b93498" :question/problem "A rehabilitation aquarium initially contains $102$ animals, and its maximum capacity is $140$ animals. Let $M(t)$ be the number of animals, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. Its population obeys an inhibited growth model. The rate of increase equals $\\frac{1}{3}$ times the difference between the maximum capacity and the current population. Which differential equation models this population?" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of animals $M(t)$ in the rehabilitation aquarium changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the current number of animals. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-M),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=140$ animals, and $k=\\frac{1}{3}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}(140-M).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537197 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "093a2862-55d4-5146-a216-0fe0ce1b00a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=2(452-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537136} {:answer/id #uuid "0e0d19fd-ebd6-5874-8433-a7c35f474597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{904}{5}-B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537138} {:answer/id #uuid "72cccbdd-18fb-5044-a13c-176b631301a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{5}(452-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537166} {:answer/id #uuid "74f95c36-2e1a-526e-9f6a-e0843a7c4a34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{5}(21-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537169} {:answer/id #uuid "cd5996f3-e86d-503b-b0a8-857d5c750856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{5}{2}(452-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537191}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537166} :answer-field/id #uuid "933c700c-2f90-5f96-9512-88ab742c3097" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f0550978-2a47-57ef-b994-adcde267b148" :question/problem "A laboratory incubator initially contains $21$ cultures, and its maximum capacity is $452$ cultures. Let $B(t)$ be the number of cultures, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in days. Its population obeys an inhibited growth model. The rate of increase equals $\\frac{2}{5}$ times the difference between the maximum capacity and the current population. Which differential equation models this population?" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of cultures $B(t)$ in the laboratory incubator changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the current number of cultures. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-B),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=452$ cultures, and $k=\\frac{2}{5}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{5}(452-B).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "496974f1-cd7b-5abe-b03b-8caf3c2ef7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541574}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541574} :answer-field/id #uuid "6df6c47b-c813-591d-800c-7da989a956a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "316d8b52-05f5-5660-9987-6f2357a674ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-342789" :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+2x}$ at the point where $x=1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-{x}^{2}+2x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2x+2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-2x+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{-2x+2}{2\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={\\left(\\frac{-2x+2}{2\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+2x}}\\right)|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2(1)+2}{2\\sqrt{-{1}^{2}+2(1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "365d6570-d8cb-539f-a88f-8f59a136deaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{145}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541535} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e65a94-bc73-5511-b4e0-737b7fd4a52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541771} {:answer/id #uuid "ba942dc9-148d-5089-895a-5986590e89c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{289}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541806} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa31f6a-0231-55ef-b7c1-eda435cc6895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{73}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541897} {:answer/id #uuid "fb45a9d2-d3cd-5774-a7e6-b9653552ab14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541949}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541806} :answer-field/id #uuid "02bb9c7f-0a51-5ad4-a60d-65bfb06add50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ed421aa6-2e1d-544f-a145-c01b981f6e83" :question/math-academy-id "q-20181" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-3}+3{x}^{3}$ at the point where $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nLet $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-3}.$ We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=x-3.$ Then $f=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-3}}+9{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=4} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4-3}}+9(4)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+144 \\\\  & =\\frac{289}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "317cc575-d6d9-5bf7-b402-e9f6f2aece5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543849} {:answer/id #uuid "37d68e3d-f8d6-5f13-a869-d9cdc65b796c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543852} {:answer/id #uuid "7693e799-17b4-580e-85ba-48cd63b488fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}$ and $\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543867} {:answer/id #uuid "e77191fb-4308-5ae9-92dc-890558829810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{4}$ and $\\frac{2}{9}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543899} {:answer/id #uuid "f6547e1e-09da-5e3a-9109-8fd09fcf5409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}$ and $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543901}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543849} :answer-field/id #uuid "6254bf33-2529-515f-a80a-8ecdfac48523" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "071541e3-e659-523c-b1f4-eade008267f7" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\frac{5}{4}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\frac{2}{9}}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{4}$ and ${b}^{2}=\\frac{2}{9}.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{4},$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=\\frac{2}{9},$ the length of the vertical radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{9}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}=\\sqrt{5},$ and the minor axis has length $$\n\\displaystyle 2b=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce4db2e6-57cc-53c9-a74c-1ffe1f592efd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{70}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543890}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543890} :answer-field/id #uuid "9736611d-7a2c-5da0-acc9-2bf3c1d58488" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543876} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbc2dfdf-cb86-5fc6-aae5-463332bc08d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{82}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543885}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543885} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6df07a4-3e4e-56fb-903b-6dca2e87c255" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3a6ef55b-f35c-5e25-8e8b-c766bf7961eb" :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\frac{82}{9}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{70}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=\\frac{82}{9}$ and ${b}^{2}=70.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=\\frac{82}{9},$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\sqrt{\\frac{82}{9}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{82}}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=70,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{70}.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=\\sqrt{70},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{82}}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{70}=2\\sqrt{70},$ and the minor axis has length $$\n\\displaystyle 2a=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{82}}{3}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{82}}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543855 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf7d248d-e685-5e26-ac25-a7926e2cbb3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543891}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543891} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa5ddd2d-b3d6-55bf-8001-4f05c1087233" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543879} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae9ca8da-f70b-516d-94a6-97e74c60595b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543881}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543881} :answer-field/id #uuid "f905f086-bf87-543a-a849-4cd7d74a502f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3f4b4f77-3a6a-59e9-8972-8eb1480893b3" :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\frac{27}{4}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\frac{3}{16}}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=\\frac{27}{4}$ and ${b}^{2}=\\frac{3}{16}.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=\\frac{27}{4},$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\sqrt{\\frac{27}{4}}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=\\frac{3}{16},$ the length of the vertical radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{16}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\n$$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}=3\\sqrt{3},$ and the minor axis has length $$\n\\displaystyle 2b=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543858 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18c52fc5-553a-58fe-913c-0513e8e57475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ and $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543846} {:answer/id #uuid "34f14618-0ece-55f3-94b9-5cfcedbab5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{9}$ and $\\frac{5}{16}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543851} {:answer/id #uuid "8944842c-7775-566d-a53d-437f30d972f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ and $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543872} {:answer/id #uuid "b0fc53f4-2541-54f0-abf9-c06a809dfe56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ and $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543882} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c9ce9b-12fd-58ae-a2ec-ed97de9e48ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ and $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543894}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543882} :answer-field/id #uuid "789ce64e-9b22-5c24-afe7-9c36ade69dd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5c19ea88-4652-5feb-b49c-a5fe7156da75" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\frac{5}{9}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\frac{5}{16}}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{9}$ and ${b}^{2}=\\frac{5}{16}.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{9},$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{9}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=\\frac{5}{16},$ the length of the vertical radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{16}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $$\n\\displaystyle 2a=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},\n$$ and the minor axis has length $$\n\\displaystyle 2b=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543859 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15738b3d-a199-50b9-9fa5-42bf040af44b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543845} {:answer/id #uuid "7639f7e6-85b3-522f-bf24-1e51f41a7916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543866} {:answer/id #uuid "84ba063b-79d0-5f5c-bf77-db44f8a5f850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543871} {:answer/id #uuid "f0cc75ef-eeea-50c8-9c25-52c64d9dd650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499